{
  "items": [
    {"n": 1, "name": "First screen", "score": 2, "reason": "The headline, decision block and lead cost claim give the reader the action (keep September), the reason (both candidates cost more at this run's routing, and neither holds character better) and the main risk (routing was never recorded, and the earlier round found qwen3.8-flash cheaper). That risk now has its own Watch item and its own next step. The previous round's must_fix on the headline and lede is resolved: both now say the costs are at this run's unrecorded routing."},
    {"n": 2, "name": "Coherent story", "score": 2, "reason": "Each claim matches its block row by row. In claim cost, the paired gaps +$0.62 and +$0.49 have intervals clear of zero. In claim character, qwen3.8-flash is at -7.6 (-10.8 to -3.4) and 1017 at -3.0 (-5.8 to +1.1), both inside the 15-point margin, and the second block shows 1017 separated worse on holding character (-4.7). The off_lane shares of 48% and 53% match measure_breakdown's loss shares. options_dominance makes September dominant, and the margin sweep holds at every margin, so the call agrees with code's reading."},
    {"n": 3, "name": "Honest emphasis", "score": 2, "reason": "The recommended arm's weak point is in plain view: the confidence line, a Watch item and a next step all say its cost advantage may belong to the routing rather than the model. The candidates' character results are not overstated. 1017's pass-rate drop is called not separated, while its loss on holding character is shown. The weakest-margin Watch line now gives both bases (12.6 corrected, 10.8 declared), which resolves my earlier finding, and the stray plus signs are gone."},
    {"n": 4, "name": "Sound elision", "score": 2, "reason": "Nothing omitted could change the call. qwen3.8-flash's story-slice drop on keeping the booth offstage (53.3% against 93.3%, ten cases) hurts an arm that is already rejected. The tool check and failed-reply notices are not separated. Measuring spend and p95 cost point the same way as production cost: the candidates' p95s are $6.20 and $8.27 against $5.30."},
    {"n": 5, "name": "Confounds handled", "score": 2, "reason": "1017's reasoning-share difference (55% against 76%) is carried on claims cost, character and off_lane (my earlier should_fix is resolved), with a Watch item and a matched-budget rerun. The model-confound clause now names the reasoning share and the unrecorded simulator settings, which resolves my earlier finding. Unrecorded routing has its own clause, Watch item and re-pricing step."},
    {"n": 6, "name": "Actionable next steps", "score": 2, "reason": "Each step names what it would settle: re-pricing at a recorded routing, with a re-entry rule; a rerun with matched reasoning budgets; judge calibration on off-lane asks; and declaring the criteria and recording routing before the next round. One wording point is noted below: the off-lane directive step reads as if no directive existed."},
    {"n": 7, "name": "Evidence fits claims", "score": 1, "reason": "The cost and character blocks show their claims' own figures, and the cost caption now explains why paired gaps differ from the gaps between means (resolved). Two claims are less clearly served. Claim examples rests on September's landlord-email and laundry failures answering the non-music ask, but its block shows September's match-score card, a different kind of failure. Claim off_lane states a share of the loss (48% and 53%), but its block draws only slice changes, from which a reader cannot get the share."}
  ],
  "findings": [
    {"claim": "examples", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The claim says September's kept landlord-email and laundry failures answer the non-music ask just as the qwen3.8-flash card does. But the September card in the block is the match-score trial, a customer-service refusal, so the claim's own point is not drawn. Show September's landlord-email failure in its place: the judge's reason is 'supplies a suggested opening for the landlord email'. That puts it directly beside qwen3.8-flash's landlord-email card. Then update the caption so it no longer sets September's card apart as a different failure."},
    {"claim": "off_lane", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The sentence states shares of the holding-character loss (48% for qwen3.8-flash, 53% for 1017), but the block draws only each slice's change (-22.9 and -20.2 points on off-lane asks). A reader can't get the share without the case weights and the overall loss. Add the loss share per slice to the block (strata.off_lane.loss_share.*), or say in the caption how the share follows from the drawn changes."},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "Method, cost basis: 'had its own missing trials cost its p95 ($2.02 per 1,000 replies)' reads as if September's p95 were $2.02. September's p95 is $5.30 per 1,000 replies, and $2.02 is its mean if its 33 missing trials cost that p95. Reword it, for example: 'had its missing trials each cost its p95 of $5.30, its mean would be $2.02, still below the candidates' floors of $2.24 and $2.36'."},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The third next step says 'Add an off-lane directive'. But the run already used the promoted directives, and the judge's reasons cite a 'directive to brush off non-music asks'. The kept failures brush off the ask and then answer it anyway (laundry 'Cold', the landlord-email opener). Reword the step as tightening the existing brush-off directive so it forbids answering after the brush-off, so a reader doesn't think no directive was in place."}
  ]
}
