    {"n": 2, "name": "Coherent story", "score": 1, "reason": "Claims cost, off_lane and examples follow from one another. Claim character does not hold up against its own block. Its sentence says qwen3.8-flash 'holds character' 7.6 points worse and 1017 is 'not told apart'. Those are pass-rate figures. The block's second forest reads the judged holding-character check: 1017 is -4.7 (-8.2 to -1.3), separated, and its caption says 'both candidates are worse on holding character'. The routing caveat also contradicts itself. It calls the measured gap 'the only cost evidence', yet the same clause cites the earlier round, which found qwen3.8-flash cheaper."},
    {"n": 3, "name": "Honest emphasis", "score": 1, "reason": "The candidates' weak axes are visible, and the weakest margin is in Watch (-12.6 against -15). The confidence line is the problem. It reads High over the routing caveat, and the page itself says cost alone decides and an earlier round found the opposite cost order for qwen3.8-flash. That caveat could plausibly be material. A cheaper candidate inside the 15-point allowed loss would be picked under the declared criteria. The High isn't scoped to what it covers."},
    {"n": 4, "name": "Sound elision", "score": 2, "reason": "None of the omitted items would change the decision. The outcome and transcript scores point the same way as the pass rate. The tool check and failed-reply notices are not separated. The p95 cost points the same way: $5.30 for September against $6.20 and $8.27 for the candidates. Measuring spend is not what a deployment pays. The story slice is 10 cases."},
    {"n": 5, "name": "Confounds handled", "score": 2, "reason": "1017's lower reasoning share (55% against 76%) is in Watch, on the cost and character claims, and in a matched-budget rerun step. The unrecorded routing is carried into Watch and the first next step. The unrecorded simulator settings are in Method and the declaration step. Method also handles the missing cost trials: the order holds under worst-case fills ($2.02 against $2.24 and $2.36)."},
    {"n": 6, "name": "Actionable next steps", "score": 2, "reason": "Each step names an experiment or a declaration and says what it settles: re-pricing at a recorded routing, a 1017 rerun with the reasoning budget matched, tightening the existing brush-off directive and rerunning off-lane asks on all three arms, judge calibration, and declaring criteria before the next round. The earlier finding on the directive wording is resolved. One gap remains: what the first step leads to if a candidate prices at or below September (see finding)."},
    {"n": 7, "name": "Evidence fits claims", "score": 1, "reason": "Both earlier findings here are resolved. The off_lane caption now explains how the 48% and 53% shares follow from the drawn drops and case counts; both figures read as stated. The examples block now shows September's landlord-email and laundry failures beside qwen3.8-flash's landlord-email card. The Method cost-basis wording is also fixed. Claim character still has a block row that its sentence rules out: 1017 separated on holding character at -4.7."}
  ],
  "findings": [
    {"claim": "c2", "severity": "must_fix", "finding": "Claim character uses 'holds character' for the pass rate on character adherence. It then says 1017 is 'down 3, not told apart'. But the judged check 'persona holds character' sits in the same block, and there 1017 is -4.7 points (-8.2 to -1.3 at 99.69%), separated and worse, as that block's own caption says. A reader takes the two for one measure, and the sentence then contradicts its own block. Fix: call the 7.6 and 3.0 figures 'the pass rate on character adherence'. Add a sentence on the judged holding-character check: both candidates are separated and worse, qwen3.8-flash -6.0 and 1017 -4.7. The decision block's 'Why not' for qwen3.8-flash should also say 'pass rate' rather than 'holds character'."},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "must_fix", "finding": "The confidence reads High over the unrecorded-routing caveat. That caveat is plausibly material: the page says cost alone decides, and that the earlier round found qwen3.8-flash cheaper. Its clause also contradicts itself, calling this run's gap 'the only cost evidence' while citing that earlier finding. Fix: scope the High to the character readings and to the cost order at this run's routing. Then say it leaves open whether a candidate costs less at a recorded production routing, which the earlier round suggests it may. Otherwise, make the form low."},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The first next step says that if a candidate costs no more than September after re-pricing, 'then character decides'. The routing caveat says the call 'would turn on character alone or become a trade'. Under the declared criteria, a cheaper candidate inside the 15-point allowed loss would be the pick: qwen3.8-flash's worst corrected drop is -12.6, and 1017's is -7.4. Say so. If the team would not accept qwen3.8-flash's 7.6-point pass-rate drop at any price, say the allowed loss should be tightened now (qwen3.8-flash stops counting as acceptable below 10.8, 1017 below 5.8). Then a reader knows what a cheaper re-pricing would actually do to the call."}
  ]
}
